{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update17.json\", \"summary\": \"Validated (validate --run <run> --submission update17.json → valid=true). Narrowed the transfer-readiness finding against its own weakest link. The readiness loop can pass on an uncorroborated replication cursor, but the harm I sketched needs TimeoutNow to actually reach the target — and the retained material contains the protective path: a transport error from the TimeoutNow call is wrapped and handed to the done channel (raft.go:1024-1030), and the leader-side goroutine forwards a non-nil done error to the caller's future instead of the nil that means 'leadership lost during transfer' (raft.go:740-744); the retained invalid-address test pins the caller-visible expectation that such a transfer must return an error naming the failed TimeoutNow RPC (raft_test.go:2518-2532). So the readiness gap cannot by itself yield the misleading success: that requires the target to be reachable exactly when TimeoutNow is sent while its cursor is still uncorroborated — the interval between the peer becoming reachable and its first AppendEntries response being processed, during which the leader's own replication loop is probing it at the same cadence. Recorded as one Fact plus a successor interpretation for the same question (earlier interpretation and counterevidence preserved), keeping the finding framed as an evidence gap in the readiness test with a stated ceiling: even if the residual window is hit, the outcome is a leadership change to some other node with a success report, not lost committed data, because a behind target cannot win the induced election. Counterevidence kept explicit, including that the invalid-address test exercises only the bogus-address case and does not construct a target returning during the window, and that the window's duration was reasoned, not measured.\"}"}}
